[
    {
        "text": "Coordinating entities in a distributed system has been a long standing problem in computing. Without a central coordinator calling the shots, how do you find a way to get everyone on the same page? This is the Byzantine General's problem in which in order to avoid catastrophic failure of a system, the system's actors must communicate with each other and agree on a single truth. Some of these actors may be unreliable. This dilemma is very applicable in computer science. Systems that can overcome the problem are said to be Byzantine fault tolerant.",
        "start": 1440,
        "end": 32620,
        "confidence": 0.9821407,
        "channel": null,
